  "theory": {
        "intro": "An **identity** is true for every value of $\\theta$. Identities let you simplify a trigonometric expression to a shorter form. The usual plan is to write everything in terms of $\\sin x$ and $\\cos x$, then tidy up.",
        "facts": [
          "**Reciprocal identities**: $\\operatorname{cosec}\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin\\theta}$, $\\sec\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos\\theta}$ and $\\cot\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\tan\\theta} = \\dfrac{\\cos\\theta}{\\sin\\theta}$, each needing its denominator to be non-zero.",
          "**Quotient identities**: $\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{\\sin\\theta}{\\cos\\theta}$ when $\\cos\\theta \\neq 0$, and $\\cot\\theta = \\dfrac{\\cos\\theta}{\\sin\\theta}$ when $\\sin\\theta \\neq 0$.",
          "**Pythagorean identity**: $\\sin^{2}\\theta + \\cos^{2}\\theta = 1$, which rearranges to $1 - \\sin^{2}\\theta = \\cos^{2}\\theta$ and $1 - \\cos^{2}\\theta = \\sin^{2}\\theta$.",
          "**Secondary Pythagorean identities**: $1 + \\tan^{2}\\theta = \\sec^{2}\\theta$ and $1 + \\cot^{2}\\theta = \\operatorname{cosec}^{2}\\theta$, so $\\sec^{2}\\theta - \\tan^{2}\\theta = 1$ and $\\operatorname{cosec}^{2}\\theta - \\cot^{2}\\theta = 1$.",
          "The algebra is ordinary: factorise common terms, expand brackets, use the difference of two squares, and put fractions over a common denominator."
        ],
        "steps": [
          "Rewrite reciprocal and quotient functions in terms of $\\sin x$ and $\\cos x$",
          "Combine fractions using a common denominator or expand algebraic brackets",
          "Apply Pythagorean identities to substitute squared terms",
          "Cancel common factors to state the simplest form"
        ]
